  <title>2.10.6: Writing Inequalities to Represent a Situation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00553</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.10.6: Writing Inequalities to Represent a Situation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e16f91e2-3acc-4746-bbfb-34912d236707</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<para>Use the following scenerio for questions 1 - 2.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Lin’s job pays $8.25 an hour plus $10 of transportation allowance each week. She has to work at least 5 hours a week to keep the job, and she can earn up to $175 per week, including $10 to cover transportation per week.</para>
</blockquote>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Represent this situation mathematically. If you use variables, specify what each one means.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(8.25h\;+\;10\;\leq\;175\) and \(h\;\geq\;5\), where \(h\) represents the number of hours Lin works in a week.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many hours per week can Lin work? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>
    <para>At least 5 hours and at most 20 hours (or \(5\leq\;h\;\leq20\)). For example: The maximum amount she could earn, not including the transportation allowance, is $165. That amount is equal to 20 hours of work, \(\frac{165}{8.25}=20\)."</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
